There’s a real principle in that, but I’d phrase it more precisely:

If you continually signal that you don’t value, choose, or care for yourself, you make it harder for someone else to know how to value you well.

That doesn’t mean you have to love yourself perfectly before anyone can love you. People can see worth in you that you temporarily can’t see yourself.

But “wanting yourself” changes your posture. You invest in yourself. You protect your standards. You become interested in your own future. You stop asking another person to supply the entire proof that you’re worth choosing.

And that is attractive for a deeper reason than confidence: you’re no longer offering yourself as something you’ve already rejected.

Maybe the sharper version is:

Be someone you would choose, then choose yourself first.